  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nThrough this evening, showers ahead of now Post-TS Rachel moved \nthrough the region, bringing light totals (less than 0.10\") for most \nof the coastal basin, with higher totals in the San Diego inland \nvalleys(0.10-0.25\") and SD County mountains (0.25-0.50\"). The storms \nlow level circulation is now tens of miles off the coast of Baja \nCalifornia and is expected to reach land around 10PM to midnight \nnear Ensenada. \n\nPost-TS Rachel is now fully embedded within the upper \nlevel trough and the rainfall core, out ahead of the core, is \nactively decoupling with the low level circulation. As of 9PM, the \nrainfall core has moved into southern San Diego County and winds are \nincreasing out of the east along the higher SD Co. coast facing \nslopes with gusts 25-50 mph. Farther east into Imperial County, \nclusters of thunderstorms are forming in a CAPE environment of 500-\n1000 J/kg. These thunderstorms have the potential to move into the \nCoachella Valley and desert slopes through around 2 AM yielding to \nrain rates above 0.50\"/hr and thus increased risk for urban and \nflash flooding. \n\nBy around 2 AM, the most widespread heavy rain bands start moving \nthrough the region from south to north. Models indicate widespread \nrain rates across all lower elevation areas of 0.2-0.5\"/hr with \npockets of more intense rain possibly over 1\"/hr through mid-morning \n(~8-11AM). Areas receiving such rain rates are at an increased risk \nfor urban and flash flooding; remain alert and heed advice of local \nofficials. Simultaneously, northerly winds west of the foothills \nwill increase throughout the night, gusting 15-35 mph (highest at \nthe coast) through around 3-5AM Sunday. Easterly winds over the \ncoastal foothills through the desert slopes will also increase \nsignificantly over the next several hours, from south to north, with \nwind gusts 45-65 mph, locally to 75 mph through passes and exposed \nridges.\n\nThe strongest winds move through around 4/5 AM through 10 AM through \nSD County (~7AM-12PM for northern areas) as Post-TS Rachel makes a \nclose approach. Wind gusts will increase to 25-45 mph across lower \nelevations and winds abruptly turn westerly and increase even more \nover the mountains, passes and desert slopes with gusts 50-80 mph \n(locally higher). By the afternoon, Post-TS Rachel will have moved \nnorth out of the region very fast. Precipitation scatters to showers \nacross the lower elevations with more persistent (though weakening) \nshowers and wind over the foothills and mountains for a few hours \nlonger (through ~2-4PM). By evening, winds will be significantly \nreduced everywhere. Isolated showers continue for lower elevations, \nremaining scattered over the foothills/mountains through Monday \nmorning. During the day Monday, the trough axis moves through, \nbringing a much more traditional round of light rainfall of around \n0.05-0.25\" through the coastal basin, with higher amounts in the \nmountains (light snow above ~8,000 feet) and rain shadowing the \ndeserts (less than 0.05\" and breezy downslope winds). Shower \nactivity looks to persist until Tuesday morning before clearing out \nTuesday afternoon.\n\nAs conditions clear out, morning temperatures Tues/Wed will drop \nnoticeably inland into the low 50s/upper 40s and in the mountain \nvalleys down into the low 30s and 20s. \n\nHigh temperatures steadily rebound, returning to 80s across the \nlower elevation by Thursday and possibly into the 90s for coastal \nareas Fri/Sat courtesy of offshore winds.",
